# Line endings are pinned HERE, not left to each machine's `core.autocrlf`.
#
# Without this file the working-tree bytes depend on whoever cloned the repo,
# which cost us twice:
#
#   1. Phantom dirty files. Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so
#      a file stored LF checks out CRLF; the moment any tool writes LF back,
#      `git status` reports it modified while `git diff` shows nothing. That
#      has repeatedly blocked releases with a change that does not exist.
#
#   2. A real, Windows-only test failure. The .cva fixtures below are compiled
#      in with include_bytes! and sha256-checked against their manifest.json
#      (crates/conva-core/tests/cva_v1_fixtures.rs). CRLF conversion changes
#      those bytes, so the hashes stop matching and `cargo test -p conva-core`
#      fails on Windows — invisible in CI, which runs Core on ubuntu only.
#
# Default: normalise to LF in the repo AND check out LF everywhere. There are
# no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files here, so nothing needs CRLF in the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never inspected or converted.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.webp binary
*.woff2 binary

# Byte-exact by contract. `-text` is stronger than `eol=lf`: it tells git to
# leave these bytes completely alone rather than to convert them to LF.
#
# .cva fixtures — hashed byte-for-byte, see note 2 above.
crates/conva-core/tests/fixtures/** -text
# patch-package input (postinstall) — mixed EOL by nature; normalising it
# would silently break the html2canvas patch.
patches/** -text
